<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">International Security Assistance Force</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/2001">2001</a>–<a href="/years/2014">2014</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a> · Also known as ISAF</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The International Security Assistance Force was a multinational military mission in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a> set up by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations-security-council">United Nations Security Council</a> in December 2001, first to secure <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabul">Kabul</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nato">NATO</a> took command in 2003 and extended it across the country, where it fought the Taliban insurgency alongside Afghan forces, with some 130,000 troops at its peak. It ended in 2014 and was followed by a smaller training mission.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/international-security-assistance-force.jpg" alt="International Security Assistance Force" width="320" height="315" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: North Atlantic Treaty Organization · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Seal_of_the_International_Security_Assistance_Force.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The International Security Assistance Force (ISAF) was established by United Nations Security Council Resolution 1386, under the Bonn Agreement, which outlined the creation of a permanent Afghan government after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> invasion of October 2001. Its first mandate was to secure <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabul">Kabul</a> and its surroundings so that the Afghan Transitional Administration headed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamid-karzai">Hamid Karzai</a> could form. The first headquarters was based on the British 3rd Mechanised Division under Major General John McColl and arrived in December 2001. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a> took command in June 2002. Under the resolution, participating states bore the costs through a trust fund to which all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> member states were encouraged to contribute.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>For almost two years the mandate did not go beyond <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabul">Kabul</a>. NATO took command on 11 August 2003 at the request of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> and the Afghan government, its first deployment outside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a>. On 13 October 2003 Resolution 1510 opened the way to a wider role, and the German Bundestag voted on 24 October to send troops to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kunduz">Kunduz</a>, the first ISAF soldiers outside Kabul.</p><p>ISAF then grew in four stages: to the north, completed in October 2004, to the west in 2005 and 2006, to the south on 31 July 2006, and to the east on 5 October 2006, when it took responsibility for the whole country. Its strength rose from about 5,000 in 2003 to some 31,000 in October 2006. After 2006 the Taliban insurgency intensified, especially in the south, and British, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a>, Canadian and Danish soldiers in Helmand, Uruzgan and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kandahar">Kandahar</a> came under almost daily attack. A tactical directive of General Stanley A. McChrystal in July 2009 ordered steps to minimize civilian casualties.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>At its peak, between 2010 and 2012, ISAF had 400 military bases and roughly 130,000 troops, and forty-two countries contributed, including all 30 members of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nato">NATO</a>. By 2010 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> provided most of the troops, followed by the United Kingdom, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. Its aim was to transfer security responsibility to Afghan forces, and it ceased combat operations and was disbanded in December 2014. Some troops stayed in a supporting and advisory role in its successor, the Resolute Support Mission.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “International Security Assistance Force” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 2016 about 700 troops were stationed abroad: 100 with the NATO-led <b>ISAF</b> force in Afghanistan, 210 in Kosovo under KFOR and 160 in Bosnia and Herzegovina.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hungary">Hungary · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Abroad it has kept 36,000 troops in Northern Cyprus since 1974, supported the coalition in the First Gulf War, served in <b>ISAF</b>, KFOR and Somalia, and holds bases in Albania, Iraq, Qatar and Somalia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-turkey">Republic of Turkey · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>It depended on foreign aid and NATO forces, including the <b>ISAF</b> mission.</blockquote><a href="/state/islamic-republic-of-afghanistan">Islamic Republic of Afghanistan</a></li></ul></section>
